The correct reduction is \(n+3\mid n^2+45 \iff n+3\mid 54\), since \(n\equiv -3\pmod{n+3}\). With \(n\ge1\), \(n+3\ge4\), so \(n+3\in\{6,9,18,27,54\}\), giving \(n\in\{3,6,15,24,51\}\). Their sum is \(3+6+15+24+51=99\).

The proposed solution introduces false claims: \(61\) is not divisible by \(7\), \(12\) is not a divisor of \(54\), \(n=10\) does not satisfy the condition, and there is no “less than 30” restriction in the problem. So the final answer \(67\) is incorrect.

